WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.615 --> 00:00:06.490
KARIM LAKHANI: So tell
us what Flagship does?

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:07.323
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Yeah.

00:00:07.323 --> 00:00:15.830
So Flagship is first and foremost
a life science innovation company.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:20.390
We have our own way by which we
innovate, which I'll tell you about.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.310
Once we come up with something
that's worth taking forward,

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.168
we form a company to take that forward.

00:00:28.168 --> 00:00:29.960
And so we also have to
figure out how to do

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.810
venture creation in a systematic way.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.080
We have our own capital that
we deploy into these companies

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.900
to help them grow for the
first two, three years.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.740
So up until then they operate as
wholly owned pieces of Flagship.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.180
And then we spin them out.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.320
And we spin them out, and for the first
time, additional capital comes in.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.550
But by that time, the culture,
the senior leadership team,

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.870
the early proof of concept, the early
kind of customer or partner traction

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.310
is there.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.800
And it looks like a company ready to
eventually live its life on its own.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.010
So we're not interested in building
kind of subsidiaries, open-endedly.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:11.450
Every single thing we do ends up
becoming a company or nothing.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:16.940
So we've become kind of a creator of
companies around internal innovations.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.880
Now sometimes these innovations
draw from external science.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.110
And I can describe that.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:25.700
But we're not only trying to
find science and then find what

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.230
to do with it and create companies.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.730
That's become in vogue
for the last few years.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.310
We've been doing this for 18 years.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.970
But we've ended up in a place
where we think the sustainable

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:39.320
way to do this is to have your own
innovation capacity, your own company

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.610
creation capacity, initial growth, and
then the companies have the wherewithal

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.473
to grow and compete and thrive.

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:47.890
KARIM LAKHANI: That's fascinating.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.230
So walk us through one
example of a company

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.780
that you guys have given
birth to from the start

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.720
to it's leaving the Flagship fold.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.970
NOUBAR AFEYAN: There are many.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.957
I think we've been
involved in doing about 50

00:02:00.957 --> 00:02:03.290
of these that are based on
internally developed science.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.900
And there's a whole bunch more
that also include external.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.509
But I'd say one of the
internally developed ones

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:11.480
that just recently went public, I
can give as an example is Moderna.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.840
So Moderna was-- and I'll tell you.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.810
We have this fairly, let's
say, prescribed process.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.330
Which the way people think about
entrepreneurship and innovation,

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.930
especially breakthrough
innovation, is that it is unruly.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:27.830
It is chaotic.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.300
It is inspired.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:29.500
KARIM LAKHANI: Serendipity!

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.310
NOUBAR AFEYAN: In my view,
it's none of those things.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:31.730
KARIM LAKHANI: None of those?

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.688
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Absolutely
none of those things.

00:02:33.688 --> 00:02:36.140
I have a completely
contrarian view of that.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.720
And I've done it enough times
in enough different fields

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.030
that I think, frankly, just
to be a little controversial,

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.460
I think that's an illusion.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.830
In other words, I think that because
people haven't done that thing before,

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.700
it's exciting and unexpected to them.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.990
But actually, pretty quickly, it
looks like just another version

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.400
of what came before them.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.830
Because the historians come
and say, well, you know,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.140
before there was the
iPad, there was the iPod.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.600
And before that, there was
the Walkman, et cetera.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.850
And also, when you look at it that way,
you go, well, why were you surprised?

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.380
And that's when you realize that
these things are just evolving.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.250
And I'll give you an example
in this company called Moderna.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.500
But before that, I'll
tell you that underlying

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.840
how we think you can
do this systematically

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.720
is the notion that breakthroughs
aren't made by people.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:25.670
They emerge.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:29.030
And the most understood,
not well understood,

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.390
but understood version of emergence
that we know about is evolution.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.620
Which is that if you do
variation, iteration, selection

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:42.000
and iteration systematically,
the winners will emerge.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.230
And when they do, they
will look surprising.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.690
But actually, they're the
result of that process.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:51.453
They're not some inspired thought that
has nothing to do with that process.

00:03:51.453 --> 00:03:53.120
So hold that thought as I give examples.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.620
They'll all be examples of that.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.760
And that actually does cause
one to think differently

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.540
about being possessive about all these
companies and all these innovations.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.440
We're not.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.140
We don't go around
saying we invented this.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.450
We may file a patent
and get an invention.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.170
But where these ideas come
from are very emergent.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.460
So in Moderna's case,
talking about emergence,

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.120
we understood by following the
various scientific advances

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.740
that people were playing around with
converting fibroblast cells into what's

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.740
called induced pluripotent stem cells.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.850
Somebody won a Nobel
Prize recently for this.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:28.290
These are called Yamanaka factor.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.310
So it turns out that if you
take differentiated cells,

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.640
and you introduce a couple, three
different proteins, four of them

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.940
specifically, these cells
can de-differentiate and look

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.950
like what came before them, stem cells.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.990
They're not normal stem cells.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.000
But they're close to being stem cells.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.260
And so why do that?

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.190
Because then you could differentiate
it and make other cells.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.440
So that was the general field.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:52.510
And in that field, there have been some
people, particularly at UPenn and then,

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:54.260
more recently, at
Children's Hospital here

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.110
in Boston, who had been playing
around with using what's

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:02.240
called mRNA, messenger RNA,
to use these to go into cells

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.650
and express these proteins.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:04.720
Why had they done that?

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.210
Because it turns out this
mRNA, they could actually

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.950
get a better transformation
of these cells.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.130
And so we were aware of this work.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:15.320
And we started asking kind
of just what if questions.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.715
KARIM LAKHANI: So your team
is reading scientific papers.

00:05:18.715 --> 00:05:19.382
It's in the air.

00:05:19.382 --> 00:05:21.173
NOUBAR AFEYAN: In this
case, yeah, exactly.

00:05:21.173 --> 00:05:22.370
And we do this all the time.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:23.380
And basically, we're--

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.550
KARIM LAKHANI: You're in the midst
of this flow of knowledge being used.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.717
NOUBAR AFEYAN: It's not about reading
papers for us as much as just, I mean,

00:05:28.717 --> 00:05:32.650
we are talking to lots of people
about lots of different things.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.710
Or somebody says, hey, by the way,
you know, I just saw this data.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:38.530
And then we actually
read papers in hindsight.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:41.500
In other words, we read papers
once we have an idea of what to do,

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.100
as opposed to read papers
to get an idea per se.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.190
The signal to noise
in papers is gigantic.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.380
If you sit there and read
1,000 papers, and that

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.260
gives you three ideas,
hard to do anything else.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.240
So I'm sure that can be done, too.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:56.980
But that's not the way we approach it.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:01.150
Anyway, so long story short, we
started wondering, for lots of reasons

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:04.750
that I can't almost reproduce
because you'd have to go back to 2010

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:08.120
in the summer and whatever else
was being thought of at the time.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.990
So understand, by that
time, we had about 20 people

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:16.460
that were doing this type of activity
in various combinations for a living.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:18.790
And so it's important.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:22.120
Because these ideas kind of
get mixed and moved around.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:26.740
This is not-- one person thinking
deeply is not going to get there.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.590
So we start asking,
can you think about--

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:33.400
we said why is there no
drug that is based on mRNA?

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.520
Now if you go back 10
years, drugs were made out

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.230
of proteins and small molecules.

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:42.040
There had been an effort to
use viruses to introduce genes

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.720
into the body called gene therapy.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.700
And a person had died early on and
that whole field was shut down.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.300
And there really was no other variation
to proteins or small molecules.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.420
And there've been some efforts to
try to make, not to get too detailed,

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:59.560
but other small DNA, RNA molecules,
antisense, what's called.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.490
And we were aware of all this.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:04.678
But we're thinking, why don't people
actually use this microRNA molecule?

00:07:04.678 --> 00:07:06.220
And that's just a thought experiment.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.022
And you can go and answer that question.

00:07:08.022 --> 00:07:09.730
KARIM LAKHANI: So you
raised the problem?

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:11.530
NOUBAR AFEYAN: You raise--

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.540
you don't say, you know,
initially, we don't start with,

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:16.870
we need to cure melanoma.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:21.015
We don't start it from what good is
this mRNA that can transform cells.

00:07:21.015 --> 00:07:23.140
Because it's not good for
anything other than that.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:25.450
But we just kind of
abstract it and said,

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:29.230
is there a combination of
a solution and a problem

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:33.940
that we can identify
simultaneously around this space?

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:36.280
And if there isn't one, then we move on.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:39.100
And in this case, that's
part of our methodology.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:44.380
In this case, what we concluded was
that people had tried and failed

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.460
10 years earlier.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.140
We understood why they tried and failed.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.490
We realized that there
was no obvious fix to it.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.810
But there was a direction of fixing
that you could modify chemically

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:54.370
some of these RNA molecules.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:57.258
And they at least hinted
that if you kept doing that,

00:07:57.258 --> 00:07:58.300
there may be better ways.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:59.200
We had no proof.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.980
There was no academic literature
that said this can be done.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:04.700
Now we could have waited for that.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.700
But we probably would
have been five, six years.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:09.357
Because now, there is
academic papers from which

00:08:09.357 --> 00:08:10.690
we could have started companies.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:17.170
But our whole thing is to almost
mine this science of the future

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:21.430
to try to solve problems of today
versus mine the science of today

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:23.180
to solve problems of the future.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:25.690
And that is exactly backwards.

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:28.038
And it's not any better.

00:08:28.038 --> 00:08:30.080
It's just that you look
at the world differently.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.580
So publications give you
evidence for what you want to do.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.505
But that's not why they
publish those things.

00:08:36.505 --> 00:08:38.380
They were thinking of
a very different thing.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:39.422
You know what I'm saying?

00:08:39.422 --> 00:08:41.530
So in that regard, that's a little bit.

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:44.697
So in Moderna's case, we identified--

00:08:44.697 --> 00:08:47.530
well, the other thing that we do,
a very integral part of what we do

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:49.180
is that we don't do this in a vacuum.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:53.195
So we go out and talk to
people about our hypotheses.

00:08:53.195 --> 00:08:53.820
These are not--

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.630
KARIM LAKHANI: You're not afraid
of people taking your ideas?

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:56.380
NOUBAR AFEYAN: No.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.258
And I'll tell you why.

00:08:59.258 --> 00:09:02.050
So we use, in order to try to create
an evolution-- so we're trying

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:03.850
to create an evolutionary environment.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.590
How do you do that?

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:09.140
The scientific method uses
hypotheses and experimentation.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.540
So we actually do the same thing.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.850
We create value hypotheses.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:18.850
We basically say, I will take an mRNA
molecule, formulate it with this agent,

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.170
stick it into muscle.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:22.050
Use muscle-- none of it is true.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:23.300
None of it's been done before.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:24.850
But it's just a starting point.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.880
We then go with that
to a handful of folks

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:32.245
that are both experts and nonexperts
in the field, super important thing

00:09:32.245 --> 00:09:34.120
if you're going to
practice this kind of work

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:36.880
is that experts actually
are not the people who

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:40.600
are going to give you an idea of how
to do things dramatically differently,

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.630
because they became experts
by already doing something

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:44.740
dramatically differently.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:46.940
And now they're defending
their territory.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:51.290
So if you go to talk to somebody
and say, can this be done out here?

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:55.490
Most people we talk to, they can tell
you precisely why it can't be done,

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.250
which is really valuable from a
selection pressure standpoint.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:02.150
But not the source of kind of-- and
here is a completely different way.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:05.860
So other people, though,
who don't have expertise,

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:10.370
but have interesting creativity-- so
what we do is we take our hypotheses,

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:11.260
and it's never one.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:13.010
Because one of the
things we've learned is

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:15.320
that if you start with
one starting point,

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.470
you only locally search
the space around it.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.640
So we sample multiple different points.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:27.220
So it might be how about mRNA as a way
to make more proteins in a fermenter?

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:30.130
So around the theme, we come up
with totally different variables

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:31.390
we try to identify.

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.067
And then we put a starting point.

00:10:33.067 --> 00:10:34.150
And then we start talking.

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:37.900
And then what's been interesting and a
great learning exercise over the years

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:43.960
that we've seen is that if you
go to somebody with a bad idea,

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.920
but you actually present
it in a precise way,

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.360
they will tell you why it's a bad idea.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.320
And in that transaction,
something happens.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:54.178
They feel smarter.

00:10:54.178 --> 00:10:54.970
KARIM LAKHANI: Yes.

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:56.960
NOUBAR AFEYAN: And you
get selection pressure.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.530
And so the way our process
works is that we actually

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:01.490
do this with multiple people.

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:05.140
So when you said, aren't you afraid
they're going to take your idea?

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:06.430
What are they taking?

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:07.630
We just made it up.

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:09.978
There's zero fact to
it, literally zero fact.

00:11:09.978 --> 00:11:11.770
KARIM LAKHANI: Just
imagining a hypothesis?

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:12.380
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Exactly.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:13.540
We're just a hypothesis.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:14.248
And they know it.

00:11:14.248 --> 00:11:16.332
Because then they tell us,
what proof do you have?

00:11:16.332 --> 00:11:16.900
I say none.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:18.070
So then, they still engage.

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:22.120
Because when we talk to these folks,
and this is what we're known for,

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:27.100
we create a bit of an expert-free zone
in the sense that-- you see, look.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:30.550
These scientists and here we are
at Harvard and MIT, Stanford,

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:33.370
these people go to the
meetings of their fields.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:38.020
And they cannot say something that
does not seem unbelievably smart

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:39.320
and reasonable.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.570
Actually, I'll make the point.

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:41.950
Let me caveat.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:46.480
Reasonableness, in my view, is
like the gravitational force

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:48.220
that keeps innovation from happening.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:52.330
Because people enforce reasonableness
in the scientific world

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:54.400
on what you are allowed to say.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.470
You go publish something
in the literature that's

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:58.952
not reasonable to the
reviewers, you can't publish.

00:11:58.952 --> 00:12:00.160
KARIM LAKHANI: You're killed.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:01.240
I've done a whole study on that.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:02.115
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Right.

00:12:02.115 --> 00:12:04.750
So ask yourself the
question, why is it that we

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:08.920
think that the only innovations worth
working on start their life reasonably?

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:12.340
In fact, I can show you many
innovations that are now,

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.740
you know, accepted as real,
transformative things.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:17.410
Every one of them started
life unreasonably.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:21.850
And yet, we go look for
them in reasonable places

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:26.600
by reasonable people who are using
reasonableness filters to kill them.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.770
So because we knew that, this was
not the first thing I had done,

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:32.680
we kind of say, look, let's just
forget reasonableness for a second.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:33.490
Just think with us.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:35.170
And they tell us bad idea.

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:36.505
Somebody in Japan had tried it.

00:12:36.505 --> 00:12:37.780
The experiment won't work.

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:39.880
And we keep tweaking and
tweaking and tweaking.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:43.510
This is different than you
hear a lot in Silicon Valley

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:45.370
speak, kind of a pivoting.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:46.300
This is not pivoting.

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:48.250
Evolution is the master pivot.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:50.080
It's, like, nonstop pivoting.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:51.640
There's nothing to pivot from and to.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.440
You're just adapting and surviving.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.590
So that mindset is super important.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.200
Now what happens is,
over time, things emerge.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:00.070
Or they don't.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:04.030
But they emerge where the
next person you talk to,

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:05.590
they can't find fault with it.

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:07.308
You know that because
it frustrates them.

00:13:07.308 --> 00:13:09.850
And then they start telling you,
well, do you have any proof?

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:10.450
You go, no, no, no.

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:11.330
I just made this up.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:12.788
It's just that we've been-- anyway.

00:13:12.788 --> 00:13:16.180
And then that point is when we
go into a lab for the first time.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:18.730
And then and only then
do we test the idea.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:20.800
Now you can't test it fully.

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:25.150
You can't test it, because
it takes years and years.

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:28.450
And one of the expertise
we've developed over the years

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:31.720
is how to figure out what to test first.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:36.040
And in life science, you can't use
these words, killer experiment.

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.930
Because you know, life sciences, kill.

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:41.290
But you want to do the
killer experiment early.

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:45.220
And so figuring out what's the killer
experiment, or two or three or four,

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:49.930
that if it doesn't fail allows you
to go forward, that's the trick.

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:51.730
And we do that in our shops.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:54.400
And in Moderna's case, we spent
a couple of million dollars,

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.690
about six months doing
a set of key things

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.440
but for which we would
not have proceeded.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:04.150
So we knew the idea had morphed and
evolved and had some resilience to it.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:06.490
We went into labs and
asked specific questions.

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:09.370
Once we saw that actually some of
the things we were hypothesizing

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:12.640
could be done partially,
we then decided, OK.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.270
Let's form the best
economic value creation

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:19.120
engine known to mankind,
which is a startup in my view.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:20.050
I'm biased.

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:23.650
And then we deploy that startup
to go after that value pool

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:26.460
in as many different ways as we can.

